<!--
  SWIP Listen — HCE AID registration (Capture Vector 2).

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  F-140 — THE PPSE AID. READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING ANYTHING BELOW.
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  For four months this file registered the six card AIDs and nothing else, and
  the consequence was that **a tap on a real POS terminal produced no pop-up at
  all** — not a wrong category, not an empty one: silence.

  The reason is the order of an EMV contactless exchange. A terminal does not
  begin by asking for Visa or RuPay. It begins, every time, with:

      SELECT 2PAY.SYS.DDF01          (the Proximity Payment System Environment)

  and reads the list of applications that comes back before it selects any of
  them. Android routes host-APDU traffic **by AID**, against the AIDs declared
  in this file. `2PAY.SYS.DDF01` was not declared, so the routing table had
  nowhere to send the terminal's very first command. It went unanswered.

  SwipListenService.ppseResponse() — the function whose entire job is to answer
  that command — was therefore unreachable code. The service was never woken,
  the SELECT AID that carries our PDOL was never sent, the GPO that carries the
  MCC was never sent, and the app had nothing to show. From the user's side of
  the counter that is indistinguishable from a broken feature, which is exactly
  how it was reported.

  The AID below is `2PAY.SYS.DDF01` in ASCII hex:

      2    P    A    Y    .    S    Y    S    .    D    D    F    0    1
      32   50   41   59   2E   53   59   53   2E   44   44   46   30   31

  It goes FIRST, and it is not optional. Every contactless terminal in the
  world sends it.

  category="payment"
  ─────────────────
  These are real payment AIDs, so Android routes them under the "payment"
  category, which means:

    • SWIP appears in Settings > Tap & pay alongside any wallet app, and
    • when another app (Google Wallet) is the default, SWIP will NOT receive
      these APDUs unless it is in the foreground and has called
      CardEmulation.setPreferredService().

  That foreground-preferred call is made in MainActivity while S-03 (Tap POS) is
  on screen and released when it leaves — see MainActivity.kt. This is the
  correct mechanism and it is also the honest one: SWIP only competes for the
  NFC field while the user is deliberately looking at the Tap screen.

  requireDeviceUnlock="true"
  ──────────────────────────
  Not required for a service that cannot pay, but set anyway. A capture writes
  to the user's ledger, and a ledger that can be written to through a locked
  phone in someone else's hand is a privacy problem, not a feature.

  Note the cost, because it is a third way to get silence: with the screen
  locked, Android will not route to this service at all. That is invisible when
  SWIP is the foreground app (the phone is unlocked by definition) and visible
  only if SWIP is ever made the default payment service. Kept, and stated.

  NOTE FOR REVIEW: this service holds no PAN, generates no cryptogram, and
  answers no GENERATE AC. It terminates every exchange with SW=6985 after
  reading the terminal's PDOL response. See SwipListenService.kt.
-->
<host-apdu-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:description="@string/swip_hce_description"
    android:requireDeviceUnlock="true"
    android:apduServiceBanner="@drawable/swip_hce_banner">

    <aid-group
        android:category="payment"
        android:description="@string/swip_hce_aid_group">

        <!-- PPSE — "2PAY.SYS.DDF01". The first command of every contactless
             exchange. Without this line nothing below is ever reached. F-140. -->
        <aid-filter android:name="325041592E5359532E4444463031" />

        <!-- Visa -->
        <aid-filter android:name="A0000000031010" />
        <!-- Mastercard -->
        <aid-filter android:name="A0000000041010" />
        <!-- RuPay -->
        <aid-filter android:name="A0000005241010" />
        <!-- American Express -->
        <aid-filter android:name="A00000002501" />
        <!-- JCB -->
        <aid-filter android:name="A0000000651010" />
        <!-- UnionPay -->
        <aid-filter android:name="A000000333010101" />

    </aid-group>
</host-apdu-service>
